# Opensidian Sync CLIs @VERSION@ (Linux @ARCH@)

Plain binaries for self-hosting Opensidian Sync, for Flatpak / AppImage users
(deb/rpm packages are not built yet):

- `opensidian-sync-server` — the storage server (ciphertext only, holds no keys)
- `opensidian-syncd` — the sync user service + CLI

Both run **only** on the OpenSSL 3.5.9 FIPS provider (CMVP certificate #5586)
bundled here as `lib/ossl-modules/fips.so`; they refuse to start without it.
libcrypto is linked statically; `fips.so` stays a separate module by design.

## 1. Verify the download

    sha256sum -c --ignore-missing SHA256SUMS
    gh attestation verify opensidian-sync-@VERSION@-linux-@ARCH@.tar.gz --repo jpzk/opensidian

## 2. Run fipsinstall once per machine

    tar xzf opensidian-sync-@VERSION@-linux-@ARCH@.tar.gz
    cd opensidian-sync-@VERSION@-linux-@ARCH@
    ./opensidian-sync-fipsinstall

This runs the FIPS module's self-tests on THIS machine (`openssl fipsinstall
-pedantic`) and writes `ssl/fipsmodule.cnf` + `ssl/openssl.cnf`. Run it again
after moving the directory. Never copy `fipsmodule.cnf` from another machine.

## 3. Use

    ./opensidian-sync-server vault create --data ~/sync-data      # prints a one-time enrollment token
    ./opensidian-sync-server serve --data ~/sync-data             # 127.0.0.1:8471; TLS: --tls-cert/--tls-key
    ./opensidian-syncd setup --url http://127.0.0.1:8471 --vault ~/Notes --name laptop --enroll TOKEN
    ./opensidian-syncd status
    ./opensidian-syncd sync-now

Second device: `./opensidian-syncd setup --url URL --vault DIR --name NAME --words`
(the 12 backup words are read from stdin). `./opensidian-syncd help` lists every command.
Always start the programs through the launchers in this directory (they point
OpenSSL at the bundled provider); symlinking them into `~/.local/bin` works.

## FIPS note

The provider is the CMVP #5586 module version (3.5.9), built from the
signature-verified upstream tarball. Linux x86_64 is the architecture of the
certificate's tested operational environments, but your distribution/CPU is
very likely not one of them. **Linux aarch64 is NOT a tested operational
environment of #5586.** Nothing here is a compliance claim.

## Requirements

glibc >= @GLIBC@ (built on Ubuntu 26.04). Licences: `licenses/`. Build details: `BUILDINFO.txt`.
